.TH CCOL_LOG_FATAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_log_fatal \- emit a log line at FATAL level with a backtrace, then terminate the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_log_fatal(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a structured log line at CLOG_FATAL severity.  It then appends
a full stack backtrace.  It then stops the calling process with a call
to exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  This macro never returns to the caller.

exit() runs the exit drain of the library (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)).
It delivers what every other async logger still holds, and it lets
every gzip compression that a rotation of any logger in the process
has queued finish, the one that the fatal record itself can cause
included.  No rotated file is therefore left as a half-written \&.gz
file beside its source.  One budget of 5 seconds bounds that drain,
and it takes the lock of a logger only when that logger has a queue or
a compression to drain.  A thread of another logger that blocks for
ever, inside a write to a pipe that nobody reads for example,
therefore never keeps the process from stopping.

The same budget, counted from the start of the first fatal call of the
process, bounds the fatal record itself.  On an async logger the drain
of its own queue and batch ends by then, and every write of the record
to an output that is not a regular file (a pipe, a FIFO, a socket, a
terminal) ends by then too.  An output that takes nothing more in that
time, such as a full pipe whose reader stopped reading, loses the
record.  The library then writes one line to stderr that says so,
unless stderr is that same output, and the process stops anyway.  A
write to a regular file waits on the disk and not on a reader, and
keeps no bound.  The exit drain that follows shares the deadline, so
one budget bounds the whole stop.  The writer thread of an async logger
takes on no new work while a fatal call waits for the logger, and that
wait may run up to 100 milliseconds past the budget, so that a write
which is in progress on the logger ends and hands the logger over.

ccol_log_fatal goes past the min_level filter of the logger.  Every
other log_* macro obeys that filter.  The library always writes the log
line and the backtrace.  It does so whatever the current minimum level
of the logger is, and that includes CLOG_OFF.  Nothing therefore
silently suppresses the cause of the stop.

The library writes the log line and the backtrace inside the shared
write lock.  They therefore always appear together in the output.  That
holds when other threads log at the same time.

In CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library embeds the backtrace as an inline
"bt" array inside the JSON object.  ccol_log_error and ccol_log_alert
do the same.  In logfmt mode it writes the backtrace as continuation
lines that a tab indents, after the main record.  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG
mode it writes each frame as a separate syslog message.  That message
carries the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the main record.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  The capture
itself can fail.  It fails on a platform with no execinfo.h, and on a
transient allocation failure while the library resolves symbol names.
The process still stops.  The logger writes one "backtrace
unavailable" marker line in place of the usual output for each frame.
In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode it writes a marker record instead.  The
omission is therefore visible, and not silent.
Memory pressure can last for a time.  The content of a record can also
leave little room before the internal buffer cap.  A single backtrace
frame that the library cannot format is then omitted in the same way.
A shorter frame that follows it can still go out.  The library never
writes a frame that is incomplete, so every line that it writes is a
complete, well-formed record.  When not one frame fits at all, the
same "backtrace unavailable" marker takes their place.  In
CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library can fail to embed the backtrace at all
under such pressure.  A "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the
place of "bt", so the omission is visible and not silent.

On an async logger, ccol_log_fatal first drains everything that
earlier calls already queued.  A message that the application logged
moments before the fatal one therefore reaches the log, and does not
die in the queue.  The macro then writes the fatal record itself
synchronously.  Nothing on that path waits without a bound.  The
library skips a drain request that the queue cannot accept, and it
does not wait on one.  The batch of the writer thread can still hold
bytes that an earlier write could not deliver.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3).
Those bytes get one bounded attempt at delivery, and the library then
gives up on them.  It repairs the framing that a split record left
open, and it writes a "log record truncated: undelivered batch dropped
(records=<n> bytes=<m>): the process stopped" record for the whole
records that it gives up on, ahead of the fatal record.  It does not
hold up the stop on a descriptor that will not take them.

The fatal record is written on the calling thread.  When the logger
writes to a pipe whose reader is gone, that write fails with EPIPE,
and the process still ends through exit(EXIT_FAILURE): under the
default CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO policy it sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN first, if
the disposition is still SIG_DFL.  Under CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED the
disposition of the application decides.  See
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3).

Because ccol_log_fatal terminates the process immediately, any logger
handles that have not been closed are not explicitly freed.  This is
intentional: on program exit the OS reclaims all resources.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
if (!recovered)
    ccol_log_fatal(lg, "unrecoverable state: %s", reason);  /* exits */

if (balance < 0)
    ccol_log_fatal(lg, "invariant violated at step %d", step);  /* exits */
.fi
